diagnosis_text:
A complete, layered diagnostic sweep of the simple_bgp topology found no active or historical fault. Phase 1 (get_reachability) showed 0% packet loss on both pc1→pc2 and pc2→pc1. Phase A (infra_sweep + l2_snapshot) confirmed no ACL rules, no MAC duplicates, no ARP poisoning, and correct default routes on both hosts. Phase B (bgp_snapshot + tc_snapshot) confirmed both FRR BGP daemons are running, both eBGP sessions are in the Established state, each router is advertising its correct prefix (195.11.14.0/24 from AS 1, 200.1.1.0/24 from AS 2), and all interfaces carry only the default fq_codel qdisc with no shaping or corruption. Phase D (pressure_sweep) found no stress tools, no CPU pressure, and no flap processes on any device. Direct examination of the FRR log files on both routers shows only a short startup burst at 03:08 (including a normal BGP connection-collision retry visible as "Opens sent: 2" on router2), followed by stable 60-second keepalive exchanges and zero NOTIFICATION or FSM-transition events. Interface carrier counters on the inter-router eth0 link are zero on both sides, ruling out link instability. The BGP neighbor tables show 1 connection established and 0 dropped on both routers. Applying the intermittent-fault ruling: no flap mechanism exists, no historical trace is present in logs or counters, and the current state is healthy across all layers. The reported BGP route churn is unconfirmed and is not reproducible from any live evidence in this lab instance.
=== END ===